==========================================================================
ACT 3 — deleting the end of the record is detected too
==========================================================================

13 blocks recorded. Hash links alone cannot catch a tail deletion: a shorter
chain still links perfectly end to end. The recorded head is what closes it —
and the tail is exactly where a breaker trip and the outcome live.

after deleting blocks 5+:  valid=False
                           reason: chain is truncated: 5 blocks present, 13 were written
                           recorded head says: (13, '718cfe9d2ebbc7958c580ef1db39b2000c288394ea9944816f076bf01a0abced')

==========================================================================
ACT 4 — the provenance gate: which runs may teach memory
==========================================================================

Memory asks the ledger before accepting any lesson:

  LEARNS   resolved cleanly
           chain verified across 13 blocks; no breaker trip

  LEARNS   failed its test-gate, but finished honestly
           chain verified across 6 blocks; no breaker trip

  REFUSED  circuit breaker cut it short
           run was cut short by its circuit breaker at [implement]; its conclusions were never reached, so there is nothing verified to learn

A failure that ran to completion still teaches — those are the useful lessons.
A run cut short never reached its conclusions, so there is nothing verified to
learn from it, and its guesswork stays out of the store where it would steer
every later run.

==========================================================================
ACT 5 — and a run whose record was tampered with teaches nothing either
==========================================================================

run-demo-resolved (edited in Act 2):  allowed=False
  chain integrity failed: content hash mismatch: this block was altered after it was written

So the same chain that makes the record auditable also decides whether the run
is allowed to change future behavior. That is the ledger acting, not just recording.

==========================================================================
HONEST LIMITS
==========================================================================

This is tamper-EVIDENT, not tamper-proof:
  - Blocks are unsigned. There is no key material here, and the writer is trusted,
    so evidence forged at the SOURCE is chain-valid. Signing blocks with a per-node
    key is the mesh-native next step (DESIGN.md Uplevel), deliberately not built.
  - Rollback references git rather than reimplementing it: a block carries the
    worktree's commit hash, so the ledger proves WHICH state was verified and git
    restores it. Git is already a content-addressed Merkle DAG and works offline.
  - Replay audits a recorded run. It does not re-execute decisions; that needs every
    model call captured, which is also Uplevel.
